<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick III, Elector Palatine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1515">1515</a>–<a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Frederick the Pious · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick III, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, called the Pious, came from the Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> line and became elector in 1559. He made the Palatinate the first Calvinist state of the Empire and sponsored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> of 1563. He supported Protestants in the French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> wars of religion, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> a centre of Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-palatine.jpg" alt="Frederick III, Elector Palatine" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_der_Fromme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a>, the Pious, was born on 14 February 1515 and belonged to the Palatinate-Simmern-Sponheim branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>. He was a son of John II of Simmern. He was educated strictly in the Catholic faith at his father's court and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, but under the influence of his wife, the pious Maria of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a>, whom he married in 1537, he followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and made a public profession of faith in 1546. He succeeded his father as duke of Simmern on 18 May 1557 and became elector on 12 February 1559, inheriting the Palatinate from the childless Elector Otto Henry (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-henry-elector-palatine">Ottheinrich</a>).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Under his predecessor strict Lutherans, Melanchthonians and Calvinists had all found a place in the Palatinate, and in the summer of 1559 bitter controversy arose over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Lord's Supper</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> deacon Wilhelm Klebitz and Tilemann Heshusius. Frederick deposed both on 16 September 1559. He studied theology intensively, and after the Naumburg Convention in January 1561 fully adopted the Reformed dogmas. He invited Emmanuel Tremellius and Zacharius Ursinus to Heidelberg, where Caspar Olevianus had been since 1560. Images of saints, vestments, baptismal fonts and even organs were removed from the churches, and church revenues were applied to Evangelical purposes or charity.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a>, prepared by a committee probably led by Ursinus, became the norm of doctrine, and a church order of 15 November 1563 and a consistory order of 1564 consolidated the changes. Ministers inclined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> were dismissed.</p><p>Lutherans reacted with great agitation. In 1565 Emperor Maximilian ordered the changes annulled, and the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1566 demanded their abolition. Frederick declared on 14 May that the matter was one over which God alone ruled, and the decree was not carried out. In 1570 he introduced strict church discipline, and on 23 December 1572 he pronounced the death sentence on the Antitrinitarian Johann Sylvan. In 1562 he gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenthal">Frankenthal</a> as a refuge to Evangelicals driven from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and in 1567 and 1576 sent his son Johann Casimir to aid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick's last years were troubled by domestic worries, since his elder son Louis was a strict Lutheran and he could not hope that his work would continue in his spirit. He died on 26 October 1576, and Louis VI succeeded him. For his second surviving son, Johann Casimir, he had carved out Pfalz-Lautern as an enclave to keep the Reformed faith alive; Johann Casimir later served as regent and oversaw the return of the Reformed faith to the Palatinate. Frederick's support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> gave the German Reformed movement a foothold within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick III, Elector Palatine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1559, when the senior electoral line died out, Duke <b>Frederick III</b> of Simmern became Elector Palatine and made the Palatinate Calvinist, issuing the Heidelberg Catechism in 1563.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-simmern">Palatinate-Simmern</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern/Hunsrück</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.98,7.52&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Simmern/Hunsrück on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4722 425 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick III, Elector Palatine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5172 695h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5172 695h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.98,7.52&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
